## AMD GPU Drivers

AMD Instinct GPUs use the `amdgpu` kernel driver included in the
{{ distro_name }} kernel, so no additional kernel module is needed. The compute
image does need the GPU firmware (`amd-gpu-firmware`, from the distribution's
AppStream repository); without it, `amdgpu` cannot initialize the GPUs. The
ROCm runtime and tools come from EPEL: the runtime on the compute nodes, and
the HIP development tools on the head node for building applications. EPEL's
HIP runtime package requires the HIP compiler, so the compute image also gains
the ROCm compiler toolchain; together these packages add about 900 MB to the
image.

The distribution's udev rules give all users access to the GPU devices
(`/dev/kfd` and `/dev/dri/renderD*`), so users need no additional group
membership to run GPU jobs.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_if_set enable_amd_gpu_driver -->
```bash
# Install the GPU firmware and the ROCm runtime on the compute nodes
{{ compute_install(["amd-gpu-firmware", "rocm-hip", "rocminfo", "rocm-smi"]) }}

# Install the ROCm HIP development tools on the head node
{{ head_install(["rocm-hip-devel", "hipcc", "rocminfo"]) }}
```
<!-- ohpc_fi -->
<!-- ohpc_end -->

::: {.tip}
**Tip:** Once the compute nodes boot, `rocminfo` on a compute node lists each
GPU as an agent (e.g. `gfx908` for an Instinct MI100), and `rocm-smi` shows
their temperature, power and utilization.

When the head node has no GPU, `hipcc` cannot detect the target and builds for
a default architecture that may not run on the compute nodes' GPUs. Name the
GPU architecture and link the HIP runtime explicitly, e.g.
`hipcc --offload-arch=gfx908 -o app app.hip -lamdhip64` for an Instinct MI100.
:::
